1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's a vegetable, but it provides no nourishment.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
It was a native cure long before it became a known carcinogen.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
The first president of the United States, George Washington, grew it.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
The 18th president, Ulysses S. Grant, died from smoking it.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
For some, it's a source of communion, pleasure, and sustenance.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Others see it as pure poison.

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
It's the most contradictory of crops.

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,500
Now, tobacco on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:48,700
They feed us, clothe us, give us medicines, intoxicate us,

10
00:00:48,701 --> 00:00:51,300
stimulate us, provide us with fuel.

11
00:00:51,301 --> 00:00:53,500
The list goes on and on.

12
00:00:53,501 --> 00:00:57,500
Our relationship to what we grow is usually fairly simple.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,640
If we need a crop, we cultivate it and consume it.

14
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:05,540
End of story.

15
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:08,780
But tobacco isn't that simple, because in some ways,

16
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,020
it's consuming us.

17
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:18,180
Tobacco's many facets, from the mundane to the satisfying

18
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:22,660
to the sinister, make it a very complex crop.

19
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:25,500
For starters, it's adaptable.

20
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:27,220
It would be very hard to find a place,

21
00:01:27,221 --> 00:01:29,520
I mean, short of Antarctica or the Arctic,

22
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:31,460
where it isn't cultivated.

23
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:33,160
It's profitable.

24
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:34,700
A good average acre of tobacco

25
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:40,900
will bring ballpark figures of $3,500 an acre.

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,040
The profitability of tobacco is still the best here

27
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:46,500
of any crop we grow.

28
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:47,900
It's pleasurable.

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Oh, wow.

30
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:50,440
Actually, it smells like food.

31
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:55,140
It's got nuances, and I smell cinnamon and a little spice

32
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:56,020
pepper.

33
00:01:56,021 --> 00:01:58,020
but it's wonderful.

34
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,200
It's so good, it makes you want to eat it.

35
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:02,220
It's a tax bonanza.

36
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:04,420
The tobacco industry overall, every year,

37
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:06,660
pays somewhere in the neighborhood of $100 billion

38
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:09,400
just in taxes.

39
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:11,700
From the seed through the cigarette

40
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:15,980
and into the local municipal state in federal taxes.

41
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:17,140
It's a killer.

42
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:21,320
Smoking is the single largest preventable cause of death

43
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:23,420
in the United States.

44
00:02:23,421 --> 00:02:26,660
Tobacco is the only product, if used as directed,

45
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:29,360
will kill you.

46
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:32,220
And it's wildly successful.

47
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:35,360
Whether grown for cigars, pipes, cigarettes,

48
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:39,060
or smokeless tobacco products, 6 million tons of tobacco

49
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:43,380
are produced each year, with the US ranking fourth in production

50
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:46,880
behind China, India, and Brazil.

51
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:49,620
Tobacco is the largest non-food crop which is grown

52
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:51,780
in the world today.

53
00:02:51,781 --> 00:02:54,680
Tobacco is grown in 21 states in the US,

54
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:57,480
but most production comes from Kentucky and North Carolina.

55
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:04,220
The US crop is mostly two different types of tobacco,

56
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:07,760
flu-cured and burly, the primary tobaccos found

57
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:09,760
in US blended cigarettes.

58
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:14,260
Burly tobacco is a very high nicotine type

59
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:18,800
tobacco that grows in Kentucky and parts of Indiana

60
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,300
and Virginia.

61
00:03:20,301 --> 00:03:25,300
It's a very scrubby, bushy-type plant.

62
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:28,300
Flu-cured tobacco gets its name from the way in which it's

63
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:31,840
heat-dried after harvest.

64
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:35,040
Unlike burley, it has low nicotine content, but high

65
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:37,460
sugar levels, which enhance its taste.

66
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:43,960
Farmers start their crop in mid-February in greenhouses.

67
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:46,260
We come to the field in usually mid-April,

68
00:03:46,261 --> 00:03:49,660
and we transplant that plant into rows here,

69
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:51,800
like you see in the field.

70
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:55,100
Tobacco requires a lot of nitrogen from the soil.

71
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:59,400
Farmers will apply 2,000 pounds of fertilizer per acre.

72
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:02,440
Fertilizers will help the plant make sugars, cellulose,

73
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:05,940
and most importantly, nicotine, the addictive chemical

74
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:07,580
produced by the plant.

75
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:10,380
Without nicotine in the tobacco plant,

76
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:16,260
it would have virtually little or nonexistent commercial use.

77
00:04:16,261 --> 00:04:20,500
When a person smokes a cigarette, the tobacco is burned,

78
00:04:20,501 --> 00:04:26,080
and nicotine is distilled off into particles.

79
00:04:26,081 --> 00:04:30,760
These particles contain water, tar, and nicotine.

80
00:04:30,761 --> 00:04:34,060
And these fine particles are inhaled into the lungs,

81
00:04:34,061 --> 00:04:37,740
and then they impact or they crash into the very small airways

82
00:04:37,741 --> 00:04:39,500
in the lungs.

83
00:04:39,501 --> 00:04:41,200
When these particles impact, nicotine

84
00:04:41,201 --> 00:04:44,080
is absorbed pretty quickly into the bloodstream

85
00:04:44,081 --> 00:04:46,920
goes into the heart and then goes right out to the body,

86
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:49,080
including to the brain.

87
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:54,080
It only takes about 15 seconds from taking a puff on a cigarette

88
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:57,120
to when nicotine has its effects on the brain.

89
00:04:57,220 --> 00:04:59,720
There's a concept of rapid reinforcement

90
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:01,740
makes a drug more addictive.

91
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:03,740
You know right away what the effect is.

92
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:05,740
You can adjust the dose.

93
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:07,940
And so smoking is actually the most addictive way

94
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:09,740
of taking a drug.

95
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:12,000
But before the tobacco can be smoked,

96
00:05:12,001 --> 00:05:16,440
the plants must mature, growing to be 42 inches high in 60 to

97
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:17,340
70 days.

98
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:23,340
At this point, they will be harvested gradually.

99
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:27,520
We go out every week or two, and we'll cut off two or three

100
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:30,420
or four leaves each time we go through.

101
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:34,060
We will end up at the top, which is the best, the highest

102
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:37,720
nicotine, the best plant that the companies want for making

103
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:39,920
cigarettes with.

104
00:05:39,921 --> 00:05:43,120
The leaves must be dried before they can be sold.

105
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:48,560
We call these bulk barns for drying tobacco.

106
00:05:48,660 --> 00:05:51,400
The curing process from start to finish

107
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:53,660
usually take about seven to eight days.

108
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:58,640
We'll yellow the leaf up at about 95 to 105 degrees,

109
00:05:58,740 --> 00:06:02,080
depending on the outside temperature.

110
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:04,320
Therein lies the term brightly.

111
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:07,440
Once that color is held, then the tobacco

112
00:06:07,441 --> 00:06:10,340
Then the tobacco is subjected to a pretty intense heat,

113
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:13,180
up to finally 160 degrees.

114
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:15,980
And that's where it stays until all of the moisture

115
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:20,780
has gone out of the leaf, and especially the stems.

116
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:24,060
The tobacco will be stored in 750-pound bales

117
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:26,300
until the tobacco company is ready to receive it.

118
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,240
Worldwide, 5.5 trillion cigarettes

119
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:34,340
are smoked each year.

120
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,440
It's a staggering number.

121
00:06:36,441 --> 00:06:39,340
But it begins mundanely enough with a seed planted,

122
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:43,240
a leaf cut from a stalk, as it has for thousands of years.

123
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:51,460
Historians believe that tobacco was first cultivated

124
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:56,700
in the Andes, sometime between 5000 and 3000 BC.

125
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,900
In South America, the tobacco was

126
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,340
used in a number of ways which would dazzle us now.

127
00:07:02,440 --> 00:07:06,100
I mean, smoking was only one option of taking it.

128
00:07:06,101 --> 00:07:08,600
People sniffed it, they smoked it,

129
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:10,300
they smeared it all over their bodies,

130
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,400
they smeared it on other people's bodies,

131
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:16,840
they drank it in a paste, and they even used it as an enema.

132
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:19,640
Shamans, spiritual leaders and healers,

133
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:22,440
used it to diagnose illness.

134
00:07:22,540 --> 00:07:24,560
They would consume enough, really,

135
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:26,360
to take them to the edge of death,

136
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:28,260
and that would take them into the spirit world,

137
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:30,900
where they could thereby examine the problems

138
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,100
within their patients.

139
00:07:34,101 --> 00:07:37,700
But it wasn't until Columbus reached Cuba on October 28,

140
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:43,480
1492, that Europeans discovered not the gold they sought,

141
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:46,280
but tobacco.

142
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:48,380
When Columbus sent some of his lieutenants

143
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:50,980
to investigate inland and try and find a little more

144
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:54,280
about the people who inhabited it and what their habits were,

145
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:56,220
one of the first matters they reported back

146
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:58,200
was that these people were smoking.

147
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:02,420
And to them, it was an absolutely surreal habit.

148
00:08:02,421 --> 00:08:03,580
They had no idea.

149
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:05,520
They had no precedent for it in Europe.

150
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:12,060
They came across a tribe who were smoking very large cigars.

151
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:14,860
Tobacco quickly gained new converts.

152
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:18,760
Its smoke wafted easily across cultural barriers.

153
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:21,580
Spanish and Portuguese sailors picked up the habit

154
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:25,180
and brought tobacco to Europe, where, ironically enough,

155
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:28,020
it developed a bogus reputation as a wonder cure

156
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:31,480
for all sorts of ailments.

157
00:08:31,481 --> 00:08:34,480
Once the British began visiting North America, they too took up

158
00:08:34,481 --> 00:08:38,480
smoking as a recreational habit.

159
00:08:38,481 --> 00:08:41,480
John Rolfe is credited as the first English settler to

160
00:08:41,481 --> 00:08:44,480
successfully raise tobacco for commercial use in Jamestown,

161
00:08:44,481 --> 00:08:50,480
Virginia in 1612. Since its inception, the colony

162
00:08:50,481 --> 00:08:53,480
had been beset by disease and starvation.

163
00:08:53,481 --> 00:08:57,480
John Rolfe realized that there could be a potential market

164
00:08:57,481 --> 00:09:00,480
for this tobacco that the natives were growing along the

165
00:09:00,481 --> 00:09:02,480
growing along the James River.

166
00:09:02,580 --> 00:09:06,580
Actually, that is what turned our failure,

167
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:09,080
our fourth failure in settling this continent,

168
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:11,020
into a success.

169
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:14,520
Tobacco secured the survival of the fledgling colonies.

170
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:16,620
By the end of the 17th century,

171
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:19,340
England was importing more than 20 million pounds

172
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:21,940
of colonial tobacco per year.

173
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:25,540
We became a tobacco society because tobacco was currency.

174
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:26,740
It paid for the preachers.

175
00:09:26,840 --> 00:09:27,840
It paid taxes.

176
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:29,040
It bought wives.

177
00:09:29,041 --> 00:09:34,440
Tobacco was the dollar or the euro of the English settlements

178
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:36,140
in the United States.

179
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:39,180
And tobacco also paid for slaves.

180
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:42,280
The slaves brought into Jamestown in 1619

181
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:46,700
were brought to help in the cultivation and harvesting

182
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:49,200
and marketing of tobacco.

183
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:51,700
In the 18th century, colonial tobacco

184
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:55,300
farmers, among them George Washington and Thomas Jefferson,

185
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:58,040
struggled as inequities in the tobacco trade left them

186
00:09:58,041 --> 00:10:01,640
permanently indebted to British merchants.

187
00:10:01,740 --> 00:10:05,440
In tobacco-producing areas, the American Revolution became known

188
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:10,780
as the Tobacco War. And in 1776, Benjamin Franklin

189
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:13,880
used 5 million pounds of Virginia tobacco to

190
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:17,400
collateralize a loan from France to help finance the American

191
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:21,400
Revolution. But whether the world was at war

192
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:25,400
or at peace, as long as there were ships on the sea, tobacco

193
00:10:25,401 --> 00:10:30,300
tobacco found new customers. Tobacco use spread through Europe

194
00:10:30,301 --> 00:10:33,040
and then beyond because it was carried there by the ships of

195
00:10:33,041 --> 00:10:36,840
European nations down to Africa, across the Mediterranean, into

196
00:10:36,841 --> 00:10:40,840
the Levant, into Turkey. It spread up the rivers through

197
00:10:40,841 --> 00:10:43,960
into Russia. From Russia, it went to China, and it was also

198
00:10:43,961 --> 00:10:50,020
carried there by the ships of the Portuguese. In the 19th

199
00:10:50,021 --> 00:10:53,860
century, tobacco use continued to spread and even crossed

200
00:10:53,861 --> 00:10:56,700
During the Civil War, the South had tobacco,

201
00:10:56,800 --> 00:10:59,760
the North had coffee and food, and many, many times,

202
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:03,040
the Yank and the rebel would meet in the middle of a path

203
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:06,080
after a battle and switch tobacco for coffee

204
00:11:06,180 --> 00:11:07,540
or tobacco for food.

205
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:11,680
Tobacco in many forms has been exchanged

206
00:11:11,780 --> 00:11:15,040
across cultural barriers between friend and foe

207
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:16,180
and for millennia.

208
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:22,780
But in the 20th century, one form of tobacco

209
00:11:22,781 --> 00:11:26,780
cigarettes would become the dominant form of consumption

210
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:31,780
and cause an ongoing health care crisis.

211
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:34,720
But what's actually inside a cigarette?

212
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:36,720
It's a lot more than just tobacco.

213
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:46,140
1.3 billion people, one third of the world's adult population,

214
00:11:46,240 --> 00:11:49,780
smoke cigarettes. Tobacco use is rising

215
00:11:49,781 --> 00:11:54,780
developing world by 3.5% per year and falling slightly in the

216
00:11:54,781 --> 00:12:00,780
developed world. There are more Chinese smokers

217
00:12:00,781 --> 00:12:04,780
than citizens of the United States.

218
00:12:04,781 --> 00:12:08,780
In 2005, Philip Morris, the world's largest cigarette

219
00:12:08,781 --> 00:12:14,780
company, generated $4.6 billion in profits.

220
00:12:14,781 --> 00:12:17,780
Cigarettes, by far the most common form of tobacco

221
00:12:17,781 --> 00:12:21,620
consumption are also the most deadly.

222
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:24,820
Unlike most pipe and cigar smokers, cigarette smokers

223
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:28,620
inhale intentionally, allowing toxins to penetrate deeper

224
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:32,620
into the body. There are different risks

225
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:35,140
associated with different forms of smoking.

226
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:38,040
Cigarette smoking is the one associated with the greatest

227
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:42,080
risk, and that is the one that's associated mostly with higher

228
00:12:42,180 --> 00:12:46,180
risks of lung cancer and mouth cancer and chronic lung

229
00:12:46,181 --> 00:12:48,580
chronic lung disease.

230
00:12:48,581 --> 00:12:50,920
But if cigarette smoking is so deadly,

231
00:12:50,921 --> 00:12:52,880
why is it so popular?

232
00:12:52,881 --> 00:12:57,120
A cigarette is an elegant form of a drug delivery device,

233
00:12:57,121 --> 00:13:01,020
almost like a hypodermic syringe and needle.

234
00:13:01,021 --> 00:13:06,740
And the delivery that one wants out of a cigarette is nicotine.

235
00:13:06,741 --> 00:13:09,280
Nicotine may be the addictive product,

236
00:13:09,281 --> 00:13:11,280
but other cigarette components that accompany it

237
00:13:11,281 --> 00:13:13,640
are actually more lethal.

238
00:13:13,641 --> 00:13:16,780
So what's inside a US blended cigarette?

239
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:20,080
Tobaccos, flu-cured, burly, and oriental,

240
00:13:20,180 --> 00:13:22,640
only make up about half the cigarette.

241
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:25,420
The rest is known as add-ons.

242
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:27,820
One add-on, reconstituted tobacco,

243
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:30,260
is made from a mixture of stripped stems,

244
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:34,420
ofl, or tobacco dust, swept from the factory floor,

245
00:13:34,520 --> 00:13:37,700
and reclaimed, cigarettes that have passed their prime

246
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:40,840
and have been sent back to the factory.

247
00:13:40,841 --> 00:13:43,580
It all goes into a giant vat.

248
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:45,140
It's beat up.

249
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:48,040
And as it's being beat up, it's constantly

250
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:49,980
extract like a tea bag.

251
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:53,820
Well, that means I removed all of the chemicals,

252
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:54,920
all the solubles.

253
00:13:55,020 --> 00:13:58,120
And then it's pooled into another vat.

254
00:13:58,220 --> 00:14:01,220
And this is a chemical reaction vat.

255
00:14:01,320 --> 00:14:05,000
The resulting solution is known as the mother liquor.

256
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:06,640
So you have this mother liquor, which

257
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:09,540
is an aqueous-based system, water-based system,

258
00:14:09,541 --> 00:14:13,940
in which now I can add a host of chemicals,

259
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:18,580
di-ammonium phosphate, urea, ammonium hydroxide.

260
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:22,720
And these additives in the recon are used to deal

