for an initial fermentation in 4,000-pound bulks.

514
00:29:02,480 --> 00:29:05,820
Under pressure in these giant piles, the tobacco will heat up.

515
00:29:06,220 --> 00:29:10,980
It could go as low as maybe 103 to 105 degrees Fahrenheit,

516
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:12,860
up to 140.

517
00:29:13,620 --> 00:29:15,980
Once that you've found this magic moment,

518
00:29:16,220 --> 00:29:18,380
you take the bulks, you get your tobacco leaves,

519
00:29:18,560 --> 00:29:20,740
and you give it oxygen. You give it air so it breathes.

520
00:29:20,741 --> 00:29:23,220
And it's releasing all these nitrates, nitrogen.

521
00:29:23,221 --> 00:29:25,460
So the starches are converting into sugars,

522
00:29:25,560 --> 00:29:28,460
and the tobacco, as it ferments, it becomes sweeter, sweeter,

523
00:29:28,560 --> 00:29:30,720
and finer.

524
00:29:30,820 --> 00:29:32,860
During a six-month fermentation,

525
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:35,460
as much as a third of the harsh-tasting nicotine

526
00:29:35,560 --> 00:29:38,760
decomposes, the tobacco's texture also

527
00:29:38,860 --> 00:29:42,480
changes from sticky to powdery, signaling that it's time

528
00:29:42,580 --> 00:29:45,680
to bale it and place it in long-term aging, anywhere

529
00:29:45,780 --> 00:29:48,320
from five to 10 years.

530
00:29:48,420 --> 00:29:51,720
After aging, the tobacco is finished in sherry barrels.

531
00:29:51,721 --> 00:29:54,360
The wood contains the nuances of the sherry,

532
00:29:54,460 --> 00:29:56,460
and the tobacco is like a sponge.

533
00:29:56,560 --> 00:29:59,660
With time, it starts absorbing all those flavors

534
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:01,860
and all those aromas from the wood.

535
00:30:01,960 --> 00:30:05,460
It's nuances like these that cigar connoisseurs seek out,

536
00:30:05,560 --> 00:30:08,580
as well as drive demand and pricing in the premium cigar

537
00:30:08,680 --> 00:30:12,980
marketplace. But would you smoke something

538
00:30:13,080 --> 00:30:18,280
called Purple Cow out of a large-mouthed bass?

539
00:30:18,281 --> 00:30:21,780
A pipe smoker would and would be happy about it.

540
00:30:27,780 --> 00:30:30,920
The pipe is probably the most varied piece of smoking

541
00:30:30,921 --> 00:30:35,420
paraphernalia. It ranges from merely utilitarian

542
00:30:35,421 --> 00:30:40,840
object to historical artifact to work of art.

543
00:30:40,841 --> 00:30:44,740
Pipes are made from clay, glass, stone, porcelain, metal,

544
00:30:44,741 --> 00:30:50,280
metal, antler, gourd, bone, shell, and briarwood, favored

545
00:30:50,380 --> 00:30:54,480
by American and European pipe makers.

546
00:30:54,580 --> 00:30:58,080
Briarwood, a root from the Mediterranean heath tree,

547
00:30:58,180 --> 00:31:02,600
has beautiful grain and ruinous flaws.

548
00:31:02,700 --> 00:31:05,100
People often ask me, how long does it take to make a pipe?

549
00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:07,400
And I say, how long does it take to make three?

550
00:31:07,500 --> 00:31:10,100
Well, I know how to make a pipe, but two of them

551
00:31:10,200 --> 00:31:12,900
are going to fall apart on me.

552
00:31:12,901 --> 00:31:15,740
This rate of failure is built into the prices of custom

553
00:31:15,840 --> 00:31:21,240
pipes, which range from hundreds to thousands of dollars.

554
00:31:21,340 --> 00:31:25,940
The custom pipe craft involves rigorous specifications.

555
00:31:26,040 --> 00:31:27,340
This is a bowl hole.

556
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:29,860
And this shows the types of clearances of wood

557
00:31:29,960 --> 00:31:31,660
that you need on each side of the bowl

558
00:31:31,760 --> 00:31:33,920
to give you the proper insulation

559
00:31:34,020 --> 00:31:36,160
in terms of the comfort of smoking the pipe

560
00:31:36,260 --> 00:31:38,900
so the bowl doesn't get too hot.

561
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:40,400
This is a draft hole.

562
00:31:40,401 --> 00:31:42,940
You'll notice that it comes in dead centered

563
00:31:43,040 --> 00:31:47,540
on the bottom of the bowl hole, perfectly executed.

564
00:31:47,640 --> 00:31:49,540
These specs are the same for all the pipes,

565
00:31:49,640 --> 00:31:51,440
including the so-called hawksbill pipe

566
00:31:51,540 --> 00:31:54,540
that Sam will fashion from briar wood.

567
00:31:54,640 --> 00:31:58,560
Now we're going to draw the pattern on the piece of briar

568
00:31:58,660 --> 00:32:00,560
that I've selected to make this pipe.

569
00:32:00,660 --> 00:32:02,760
It's a tight fit, but it's going to work.

570
00:32:02,860 --> 00:32:04,360
The first cut we're going to make

571
00:32:04,460 --> 00:32:06,300
is across the back of the shank.

572
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:07,900
And they're giving you a chance to see if I've got

573
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:09,100
any defects there.

574
00:32:10,400 --> 00:32:12,400
Looking good.

575
00:32:16,400 --> 00:32:17,400
We've lost it.

576
00:32:17,401 --> 00:32:20,400
That's the defect, and it's major.

577
00:32:20,401 --> 00:32:22,400
So now what I'm going to do is set this aside.

578
00:32:22,401 --> 00:32:24,400
There's no point in going any further with this.

579
00:32:24,401 --> 00:32:27,400
Beyond aesthetic issues, a defect in the bowl

580
00:32:27,401 --> 00:32:30,400
could allow heat from the smoldering tobacco to burn through.

581
00:32:36,400 --> 00:32:39,400
Once draft, tenon, and bowl holes are drilled,

582
00:32:39,401 --> 00:32:42,100
Sam's ready to shape the pipe.

583
00:32:42,200 --> 00:32:45,500
You'll notice there are a few other lines drawn on the pipe.

584
00:32:45,600 --> 00:32:50,580
That's to give me a visual of the profile I'm making.

585
00:32:50,680 --> 00:32:54,180
And I will use this 100-grit sanding disk

586
00:32:54,280 --> 00:32:57,880
to roughly cut this out.

587
00:32:57,980 --> 00:33:00,560
Sandings with progressively finer sandpaper,

588
00:33:00,660 --> 00:33:04,520
from 150 to 600 grit, will yield the smooth shape

589
00:33:04,620 --> 00:33:06,360
of the hawksbill pipe.

590
00:33:06,460 --> 00:33:09,400
Once stained, it's polished with carnauba wax.

591
00:33:09,401 --> 00:33:14,400
Sam Lernod produces a wide variety of pipe shapes and

592
00:33:14,401 --> 00:33:18,400
finishes. He also makes fanciful figural pipes.

593
00:33:18,401 --> 00:33:21,400
Sam Lernod, This is what I call the largemouth bass.

594
00:33:21,401 --> 00:33:25,400
You note the tail, the mouth, and I mean, there's an

595
00:33:25,401 --> 00:33:29,400
incredible amount of handwork in this pipe.

596
00:33:29,401 --> 00:33:32,400
Handwork has long been the tradition of pipe makers.

597
00:33:32,401 --> 00:33:35,400
When the English colonized the New World, they encountered

598
00:33:35,401 --> 00:33:39,600
they encountered Native Americans smoking out of handcrafted pipes

599
00:33:39,601 --> 00:33:44,400
made from carved wood and stone that were central to their daily lives.

600
00:33:44,401 --> 00:33:47,140
If a tribe wanted to declare war against another,

601
00:33:47,141 --> 00:33:49,040
it would send it its war pipes.

602
00:33:49,041 --> 00:33:52,360
If it intended to offer peace, it would send it the pipe of peace.

603
00:33:52,361 --> 00:33:55,860
And as a consequence, when smoking was introduced to England,

604
00:33:55,861 --> 00:33:58,460
it was smoked out of pipes.

605
00:33:58,461 --> 00:34:00,860
Today, if you're a pipe smoker, you probably have

606
00:34:00,861 --> 00:34:03,360
a preferred pipe tobacco blend.

607
00:34:05,400 --> 00:34:10,400
Cornell and Diehl in Morganton, North Carolina, makes the two

608
00:34:10,401 --> 00:34:16,400
main tobacco blends. There's an English blend that

609
00:34:16,401 --> 00:34:20,400
is without any flavoring on it, just the natural tobaccos.

610
00:34:20,401 --> 00:34:25,400
Then there's aromatic blends, and they have flavor on them.

611
00:34:25,401 --> 00:34:29,400
And these are our finished flavors here.

612
00:34:29,401 --> 00:34:34,400
They're all food-grade flavors. One will have abricotty,

613
00:34:34,401 --> 00:34:38,600
in it and maybe a little vanilla, and we mix these according to

614
00:34:38,700 --> 00:34:41,240
formula. Though cigarettes and pipe

615
00:34:41,340 --> 00:34:44,680
tobaccos both have food flavorings or ameliorants, most

616
00:34:44,780 --> 00:34:47,440
pipe tobaccos are not treated with chemicals to enhance

617
00:34:47,540 --> 00:34:51,520
nicotine delivery. In addition to taste and

618
00:34:51,620 --> 00:34:55,180
strength, one key component of an aromatic pipe blend is its

619
00:34:55,280 --> 00:35:00,520
room note. That's the smell of the

620
00:35:00,521 --> 00:35:05,320
tobacco in the room to the other people in the room.

621
00:35:05,420 --> 00:35:11,760
If you have a nice smell, a warm food smell, like apricot

622
00:35:11,860 --> 00:35:16,260
or maple syrup, the pipe smoker is better accepted by those

623
00:35:16,360 --> 00:35:19,180
around him. The global pipe tobacco

624
00:35:19,280 --> 00:35:22,280
market is roughly 4 million pounds a year.

625
00:35:22,380 --> 00:35:25,880
Of this, the premium market accounts for 200,000 pounds.

626
00:35:25,980 --> 00:35:28,520
This is Cornell and Diehl's market.

627
00:35:28,521 --> 00:35:30,820
You might call us a boutique.

628
00:35:30,920 --> 00:35:35,120
Many of our new customers are coming over from smoking cigars

629
00:35:35,220 --> 00:35:38,460
or are stopping smoking cigarettes.

630
00:35:38,560 --> 00:35:41,460
Pipe smoking carries similar rates of oral and esophageal

631
00:35:41,560 --> 00:35:45,680
cancer as cigar smoking, but far lower lung cancer rates

632
00:35:45,780 --> 00:35:48,880
than cigarettes, since like cigar smokers, pipe smokers

633
00:35:48,980 --> 00:35:50,080
don't habitually inhale.

634
00:35:53,380 --> 00:35:57,280
Cornell and Diehl produce around 80,000 tins a year.

635
00:35:57,281 --> 00:36:00,080
We managed to do some fun labels.

636
00:36:00,920 --> 00:36:02,520
Recent one was Purple Cow.

637
00:36:02,860 --> 00:36:04,680
We even did Old Outhouse one year.

638
00:36:04,920 --> 00:36:06,100
We have Bayou Night.

639
00:36:07,080 --> 00:36:08,940
We have Mississippi Mud.

640
00:36:10,020 --> 00:36:13,020
We've always said that if you can't take a joke,

641
00:36:13,060 --> 00:36:13,800
you shouldn't have joined.

642
00:36:16,440 --> 00:36:19,000
But what about people who want to consume tobacco

643
00:36:19,001 --> 00:36:20,660
but don't want all that smoke?

644
00:36:21,160 --> 00:36:22,920
For them, there's smokeless tobacco,

645
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:24,400
chewing tobacco,

646
00:36:25,560 --> 00:36:26,920
American moist snuff,

647
00:36:27,280 --> 00:36:31,320
And Swedish snus, products that are only consumed in the United

648
00:36:31,420 --> 00:36:33,780
States and Scandinavia.

649
00:36:33,880 --> 00:36:37,520
Swedish snus is a fine ground oil tobacco product.

650
00:36:37,620 --> 00:36:39,920
It comes in loose or in pouches.

651
00:36:40,020 --> 00:36:42,720
There are larger pouches, one gram size.

652
00:36:42,820 --> 00:36:44,240
There are smaller pouches.

653
00:36:44,340 --> 00:36:46,940
You use it between the upper lip and gum,

654
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:49,340
and it comes in various flavors.

655
00:36:49,440 --> 00:36:51,900
Swedish snus is the most discreet of smokeless

656
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:52,940
products.

657
00:36:53,040 --> 00:36:56,240
Besides being barely visible, it doesn't involve spitting,

658
00:36:56,241 --> 00:37:00,240
spitting and is used by half of Swedish tobacco consumers, who

659
00:37:00,241 --> 00:37:05,040
absorb doses of nicotine from Snus through their mouths.

660
00:37:05,041 --> 00:37:08,240
As with all forms of smokeless tobacco, users show increased

661
00:37:08,241 --> 00:37:13,240
incidence of oral cancer over non-users.

662
00:37:13,241 --> 00:37:17,240
Swedish Snus is the mainstay of Swedish Match, a nearly century

663
00:37:17,241 --> 00:37:20,240
old Swedish company that markets both premium and machine made

664
00:37:20,241 --> 00:37:24,240
cigars. Chewing tobacco, pipe tobacco,

665
00:37:24,241 --> 00:37:30,880
snuff, matches, lighters, and snus.

666
00:37:30,881 --> 00:37:33,680
What separates Americans from their Swedish smokeless

667
00:37:33,681 --> 00:37:37,880
counterparts is spitting. American favorites, moist snuff

668
00:37:37,881 --> 00:37:42,000
and chewing tobacco, are placed between the lower lip and gum,

669
00:37:42,001 --> 00:37:48,240
requiring expectoration, and are primarily used in rural areas.

670
00:37:48,241 --> 00:37:52,000
Moist snuff is a ground tobacco product, but chewing tobacco

671
00:37:52,001 --> 00:37:57,000
comes as a cut leaf. Chewing tobacco is cut

672
00:37:57,100 --> 00:37:59,640
and stripped to a certain size.

673
00:37:59,740 --> 00:38:02,880
It is then processed, and then we put what is called a casing

674
00:38:02,980 --> 00:38:06,080
that's essentially a top flavoring on the product.

675
00:38:06,180 --> 00:38:08,880
It's usually a molasses or a sugar type flavoring

676
00:38:08,980 --> 00:38:12,480
on the product. It is then dried to a specified point

677
00:38:12,580 --> 00:38:15,120
and then is put into a pouch.

678
00:38:15,220 --> 00:38:19,360
Swedish Match's chewing tobacco is called Red Man.

679
00:38:19,361 --> 00:38:22,100
It was originally produced by the Pinkerton Tobacco Company

680
00:38:22,200 --> 00:38:24,760
in Ohio, starting in 1904.

681
00:38:24,860 --> 00:38:27,060
To this day, it's still the most popular form

682
00:38:27,160 --> 00:38:29,900
of chewing tobacco that's on the market.

683
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:31,800
But no matter what your preference,

684
00:38:31,900 --> 00:38:34,700
if you're a tobacco user, there are almost as many quitting

685
00:38:34,800 --> 00:38:36,820
products as tobacco products.

686
00:38:36,920 --> 00:38:40,720
And much like consuming, how you quit is a matter of taste.

687
00:38:44,660 --> 00:38:47,420
Of all the people who smoke cigarettes, for example,

688
00:38:47,421 --> 00:38:52,420
70% to 80% or more are addicted.

689
00:38:52,520 --> 00:38:54,420
If you look at alcohol use,

690
00:38:54,520 --> 00:38:59,460
probably 5% or 10% of all alcohol users are addicted.

691
00:38:59,560 --> 00:39:01,960
And even if you look at cocaine and heroin,

692
00:39:02,060 --> 00:39:05,900
there's a lot of casual use, and probably 50% or fewer

693
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:09,200
cocaine and heroin users are actually addicted.

694
00:39:09,300 --> 00:39:12,820
So by those criteria, nicotine is the most addictive

695
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:16,340
of the various addictive substances people use.

696
00:39:16,341 --> 00:39:18,440
Once it enters the brain, nicotine

697
00:39:18,540 --> 00:39:22,640
causes the release of a cascade of hormones, dopamine,

698
00:39:22,740 --> 00:39:26,080
serotonin, and endorphins, which increase pleasure,

699
00:39:26,180 --> 00:39:29,580
reduce appetite, and improve mood.

700
00:39:29,680 --> 00:39:32,060
But nicotine also alters the chemical structure

701
00:39:32,160 --> 00:39:34,600
of a smoker's brain.

702
00:39:34,700 --> 00:39:39,560
The brain will produce more nicotine receptors that then

703
00:39:39,660 --> 00:39:41,160
need to get activated.

704
00:39:41,260 --> 00:39:44,860
So then the brain is going to want to seek more nicotine,

705
00:39:44,861 --> 00:39:48,600
and a person's gonna do that by smoking.

706
00:39:48,700 --> 00:39:51,500
Without cigarettes, smokers may experience withdrawal

707
00:39:51,600 --> 00:39:54,240
symptoms in as little as an hour.

708
00:39:54,340 --> 00:39:56,240
Withdrawal symptoms are terrible.

709
00:39:56,340 --> 00:39:58,140
People feel really rotten.

710
00:39:58,240 --> 00:40:01,380
They feel irritable, angry.

711
00:40:01,480 --> 00:40:05,220
They have insomnia, can feel depressed.

712
00:40:05,320 --> 00:40:07,220
For smokers who attempt to quit,

713
00:40:07,320 --> 00:40:09,920
there are a number of approaches to overcoming withdrawal

714
00:40:10,020 --> 00:40:11,120
symptoms.

715
00:40:11,220 --> 00:40:13,620
Some people respond better to behavioral therapy.

716
00:40:13,621 --> 00:40:18,620
therapy, some to pharmacotherapy, some a combination of behavioral and pharmacotherapy,

717
00:40:18,621 --> 00:40:22,843
and then there are those few who go cold turkey and just say, I'm going to do it.

718
00:40:22,844 --> 00:40:23,544
Not real

719
00:40:23,545 --> 00:40:27,740
common, but they do it. More succeed by developing a plan

720
00:40:27,741 --> 00:40:32,380
with a counselor. The idea with getting the counseling

721
00:40:32,381 --> 00:40:37,101
and the professional support is, okay, what am I going to do in that situation?

722
00:40:37,102 --> 00:40:37,802
When I

723
00:40:37,803 --> 00:40:42,420
feel stressed, what can I do instead of grabbing for a cigarette?

724
00:40:42,421 --> 00:40:44,980
On the technological side, there are three types of quitting

725
00:40:44,981 --> 00:40:47,260
medications. Some require a prescription,

726
00:40:47,261 --> 00:40:49,860
and some don't. The first category is nicotine

727
00:40:49,861 --> 00:40:55,660
replacement therapy in the form of gum, inhaler, nasal spray,

728
00:40:55,661 --> 00:41:02,780
patch, or lozenge. Nicotine replacement provides

729
00:41:02,781 --> 00:41:05,980
less than half the nicotine a smoker would normally get and

730
00:41:05,981 --> 00:41:10,280
tapers off over the 12-week course of treatment.

731
00:41:10,281 --> 00:41:19,520
The second category is bupropion, which is also known as Zyban, which was marketed originally

732
00:41:19,521 --> 00:41:22,640
and still is as an antidepressant.

733
00:41:22,641 --> 00:41:27,520
But it was found that people taking this drug had less craving for cigarettes.

734
00:41:27,521 --> 00:41:34,360
And it turns out that the effects of bupropion resemble effects of nicotine in the pattern

735
00:41:34,361 --> 00:41:37,160
of hormone release in the brain.

736
00:41:37,161 --> 00:41:40,260
A third category is a drug, varenicline.

737
00:41:40,360 --> 00:41:43,060
This is a totally new category of drug,

738
00:41:43,160 --> 00:41:45,840
which has got nicotine-like effects,

739
00:41:45,940 --> 00:41:48,140
but also nicotine-blocking effects.

740
00:41:48,240 --> 00:41:52,140
The nicotine-blocking effects mean that if you have a lapse

741
00:41:52,240 --> 00:41:55,780
or a slip and you smoke a cigarette, it's not as satisfying.

742
00:41:55,880 --> 00:41:59,620
Up to 30% of those who try will completely kick the habit,

743
00:41:59,720 --> 00:42:01,880
but most will take multiple attempts.

744
00:42:01,980 --> 00:42:05,080
Success rates double with counseling and medication.

745
00:42:05,081 --> 00:42:08,620
Most ex-smokers say that quitting smoking is the hardest

746
00:42:08,720 --> 00:42:11,580
thing that they have ever done in their lives.

747
00:42:11,680 --> 00:42:14,320
Smokers who do quit still have a higher risk of cancer

748
00:42:14,420 --> 00:42:18,120
than non-smokers, but far lower than smokers who don't stop.

749
00:42:18,220 --> 00:42:21,160
There are many lasting health benefits to quitting.

750
00:42:21,260 --> 00:42:23,260
Your general health will improve.

751
00:42:23,360 --> 00:42:25,200
So those people who have emphysema,

752
00:42:25,300 --> 00:42:27,040
they'll have less problems.

753
00:42:27,140 --> 00:42:28,700
Those people who have chronic bronchitis

754
00:42:28,800 --> 00:42:31,300
will have less acute infections and so on.

755
00:42:31,400 --> 00:42:33,200
Your quality of life will improve.

756
00:42:33,201 --> 00:42:36,800
So nobody ever should think, I've done it my whole life.

757
00:42:36,900 --> 00:42:38,500
I shouldn't stop now.

758
00:42:38,600 --> 00:42:40,440
Many people have little reason to believe

759
00:42:40,540 --> 00:42:43,940
that our society will wean itself from tobacco.

760
00:42:44,040 --> 00:42:47,440
I think people are going to use tobacco

761
00:42:47,540 --> 00:42:49,960
as long as they can afford it.

762
00:42:50,060 --> 00:42:53,160
I think there'll be tobacco grown right on and on,

763
00:42:53,260 --> 00:42:56,860
but the volume may change, it may decrease.

764
00:42:56,960 --> 00:42:59,860
But I still think tobacco is here to stay.

765
00:42:59,960 --> 00:43:03,200
When we see this product gone, it's not going to be

766
00:43:03,201 --> 00:43:04,200
years?

767
00:43:04,201 --> 00:43:06,200
I don't think so.

768
00:43:06,201 --> 00:43:10,700
Do I think it's going to become more regulated and constrained?

769
00:43:10,701 --> 00:43:13,200
Yes, it's happening.

770
00:43:13,201 --> 00:43:16,200
Regulated or not, tobacco has been with humankind

771
00:43:16,201 --> 00:43:18,200
for thousands of years.

772
00:43:18,201 --> 00:43:21,200
And increasingly, this contradictory crop that generates

773
00:43:21,201 --> 00:43:26,200
profits and has become a source of bitter conflict will continue

774
00:43:26,201 --> 00:43:30,200
to pit pleasure against mortality.

